            <ArticleTitle>The antinociceptive effect of systemic administration of a combination of low-dose tramadol and dexmedetomidine in a rat model of bone cancer pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>30-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/EJA.0b013e3283624a0f</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Multiple factors are involved in the mechanism of bone cancer pain. Treatment with a single drug is not adequate to target all of the different mechanisms.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To study the analgesic effect of a combination of low-dose dexmedetomidine (DEX) and tramadol (TRA) on bone cancer pain in rats.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">A randomised, controlled study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Central Laboratory of Shanghai Chest Hospital, Shanghai Jiaotong University, Shanghai China, from July 2011 to June 2012.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="ANIMALS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Adult female Wistar rats weighing 180 to 200g.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Rats with bone cancer were divided into five groups based on drug treatment (n=12 for each group): T5 group (TRA 5 mg kg), T10 group (TRA 10 mg kg), D1 group (DEX 1 μg kg), T5+D1 group (TRA 5 mg kg+DEX 1μg kg) and IS (isotonic saline 0.5 ml) group.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">The mechanical threshold and spontaneous paw withdrawal were measured in all groups.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Both the T5+D1 group and T10 group showed a significantly increased mechanical threshold and a lower incidence of spontaneous paw withdrawal compared with the IS group. Interestingly, there was no significant difference between the T5+D1 and T10 groups.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">We found that a combination of DEX and TRA at low doses provided equal or superior analgesic effects on bone cancer pain compared to high-dose TRA alone. Our animal data might indicate the clinical administration of these two drugs in bone cancer pain therapy.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
